This directory contains formalisations and SML code to lift machine
code instructions and programs to BIR programs. The main entry point
is "bir_inst_liftingLib". It provides top-level functions for
performing such liftings. Please look at its signature for details.

A large component of the lifter is the expression lifter, which is
able to construct bir-expressions corresponding to terms of word types.
You might want to look at "bir_exp_liftingLib" for using it directly.

RISC-V
------

The RISC-V instance lifts the hart procID of HOL4's L3 model of RISC-V
(priv-1.13 M-mode and U-mode with PMP, vendored with its step library in
src/shared/l3-machine-code/riscv, see VENDORING.md there).  The step
theorems describe a step of the hart in M-mode and carry the M-mode
contract of the step library as hypotheses; the lifter turns each into an
assertion of the BIR block of the instruction:

  - every instruction: priv = 3 (H1), no locked PMP entry in pmpcfg0 and
    pmpcfg2 (H3), mstatus.MIE clear (H6);
  - loads, stores, AMOs, LR and SC: also mstatus.MPRV clear (H2) and the
    natural alignment of the address (H5); for 8-byte accesses also every
    PMP entry OFF or NAPOT (H4);
  - the F and D instructions: also mstatus.FS not Off (mstatus && 0x6000
    <> 0).

These are assertions, and not part of the extra predicate of the machine
record, because that predicate must hold before and after every lifted
instruction, and instructions change all of them: MRET the privilege,
mstatus.MIE and mstatus.MPRV, and the CSR instructions mstatus and the PMP
configuration.  The extra predicate (riscv_state_is_OK in
src/theory/tools/lifter/bir_lifting_machinesScript.sml) holds only what no
lifted instruction changes: no exception, no pending control transfer,
RV64 (misa.MXL <> 1, which is read-only) and the C extension enabled
(misa.C).  Only a write of misa can change misa.C, and no write of misa
lifts, whatever the written value: the lifter proves the extra predicate
after each instruction for a symbolic pc (it places the lifted block at
its address afterwards), and after a write of misa that proof fails, in
"computing al_step and ms'", since misa.C then depends on the written
value and on the pc (the model keeps misa.C set where clearing it would
misalign the next instruction, at a pc that is not 4-byte aligned) and
the lifter does not simplify the misa record that the model writes.  So
misa.C and misa.MXL hold throughout lifted code.  With misa.C, a
compressed instruction, a jump or branch to an address with bit 1 set, and
the jump of MRET (whose target the model masks to 4 bytes when misa.C is
clear) need no assertion.

The lifted state is listed in riscv_bmr there: the general-purpose and
floating-point registers, the memory, the privilege ("priv", 8 bits: 3 for
M-mode, 0 for U-mode), the CSRs mstatus, mtvec, mie, mip, mscratch, mepc,
mcause, mtval, mhartid, mcounteren, mcountinhibit, scounteren, pmpcfg0,
pmpcfg2 and pmpaddr0 to pmpaddr15 (mstatus, mie, mip and mcause as the
64-bit words of their registers, so that their fields are bits of the BIR
variables), the hart number ("procID", 8 bits) and the external interrupt
lines ("extMEIP", "extSEIP", booleans).  No instruction changes the last
three.

The privileged instructions are lifted to the following blocks, after the
assertions:

  - ECALL, EBREAK and C.EBREAK take a trap to M-mode (NextRISCV_trap of
    the step library; delegation does not apply to traps from M-mode):
    priv := 3; mstatus := mstatus with MPP := 3, MPIE := 0 and MIE := 0
    (MPP is the privilege and MPIE the old MIE, which are 3 and 0 by the
    assertions H1 and H6); mepc := the pc of the instruction; mcause := 11
    (environment call from M-mode) or 3 (breakpoint); mtval := 0 for ECALL
    and the pc for EBREAK; and the block ends in a jump to the base of
    mtvec, mtvec && 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFC (where an exception goes in both
    the direct and the vectored mode).
  - MRET returns from a trap (NextRISCV_mret): priv := if MPP = 2 then 0
    else MPP (the model maps the reserved encoding to U-mode), with MPP
    read from mstatus as (mstatus >> 11) & 3; mstatus := mstatus with
    MIE := MPIE, MPIE := 1, MPP := 0 and MPRV := MPRV && (MPP = 3), so
    MPRV is cleared unless MRET returns to M-mode; and the block ends in a
    jump to mepc.  When MRET returns to U-mode, the assertion priv = 3 of
    the next lifted block fails: the lifter does not describe U-mode code.
  - WFI and SFENCE.VMA do nothing in M-mode, as FENCE and FENCE.I: their
    blocks have only the assertions.
  - "csrr rd, mip" reads SSIP and STIP from the BIR variable mip, MSIP
    and MTIP from the CLINT in the memory (bit 0 of msip at 0x2000000 +
    4 * procID; MTIP is set if mtimecmp, the doubleword at 0x2004000 +
    8 * procID, is at most mtime, the doubleword at 0x200BFF8), SEIP from
    mip or the line extSEIP, and MEIP from the line extMEIP.

A write of a CSR that the model stores but the lifter does not lift
(medeleg, mideleg, menvcfg, and of the S-mode CSRs stvec, sscratch, sepc,
scause, stval, senvcfg and satp) lifts to a block without an assignment
for it: the CSR is not part of the BIR state, and it has no effect on the
lifted code, since a trap from M-mode is never delegated and M-mode
accesses are not translated (mstatus.MPRV being clear).  A write of the
view sstatus lifts to an assignment of mstatus; the views sie and sip
depend on mideleg, so their reads and writes do not lift.

The loads, stores and moves of the D extension lift (FLD, FSD and their
compressed forms, FMV.X.D, FMV.D.X), and of the F extension FSW and
FMV.X.W.  FLW and FMV.W.X do not: they write the 32-bit value NaN-boxed,
as 0xFFFFFFFFw @@ v, and the expression lifter does not lift the
concatenation.

Instructions that read other state, or that need a step theorem that the
step library does not have, do not lift: "csrr mcycle" and the other
counters (NextRISCV does not count), reads of the CSRs that are not lifted
(misa, medeleg, mideleg, the floating-point CSRs fflags, frm and fcsr, and
so on), SC.W and SC.D (the load reservation is not lifted), writes of misa
(see above), floating-point arithmetic and conversions (BIR has no
floating-point operations, and their rounding mode, a field of fcsr, is
not lifted), SRET (the step library has no step theorem for it) and
illegal instructions.
